// CHTM Academy question bank, units 4 to 6. All questions are original management scenarios.
window.FRA = window.FRA || {};
FRA.questions = FRA.questions || [];
FRA.questions.push(
// u4l1
{id:"u4l1-1",t:"u4l1",q:"Three calls are waiting: a ventilator in use on a patient, a warming cabinet in a clinic, and an infusion pump on a unit that has two spares in place. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Assign them in the order they were received, which is the fairest method","Assign a ventilator call first, because triage is by clinical impact rather than by the order calls arrived","Send one technician to each call so all three units see a response","Ask the three callers to agree among themselves which is most urgent"],c:1,e:"Triage ranks work by clinical impact and urgency, never by arrival order. A life-support device in use outranks a convenience item, and a device with a spare already in place can wait."},
{id:"u4l1-2",t:"u4l1",q:"A pump was repaired in ninety minutes and returned to the unit without anyone telling the nurse what had been found. The unit has complained about slow service. What does this show?",a:["The repair time target needs to be shortened","The nurse should check the work order system herself","The notification stage of the work order was skipped, and an unexplained return is remembered as a delay","Satisfaction surveys are an unreliable measure of service"],c:2,e:"The work order is not finished until the user has been told what was wrong and what was done. Departments routinely lose credit for fast work by skipping the last stage of the lifecycle."},
{id:"u4l1-3",t:"u4l1",q:"A risk manager asks for the complete service record of one pump four years after a patient event. Which set of contents answers that request?",a:["The asset number and the dates the device was in the shop","A summary report of all work performed on that device type","The technician's notes and the parts invoice","Asset number, times in and out, who did the work, reported problem, findings, action, parts with cost, labor hours, test results, and the return-to-service decision"],c:3,e:"The complete record exists for exactly this moment. Without findings, test results, and the return-to-service decision there is no evidence the device met specification when it went back to a patient."},
{id:"u4l1-4",t:"u4l1",q:"Your department improves the wording of its customer satisfaction survey every year so the questions are clearer. What does this practice cost you?",a:["The ability to compare results across years, because a survey that keeps changing cannot show a trend","Response rates, which fall when a survey changes","Nothing, because clearer questions produce better data","Anonymity, because new questions identify respondents"],c:0,e:"A satisfaction survey is kept unchanged on purpose so that scores compare year to year by facility, shift, and department. Rewriting it destroys the only thing a repeated survey is good for."},
{id:"u4l1-5",t:"u4l1",q:"A review of last month shows forty work orders closed with the code cannot duplicate and thirty-one coded as use error. What is the MOST appropriate response?",a:["Reopen them, because an unresolved call is not a closed call","Trend both codes separately and look for patterns by department, model, shift, and recent changes","Remove both codes, since neither represents an equipment failure","Report them as equipment failures, since the department consumed time on them"],c:1,e:"Both codes describe real work that is not an equipment failure. Trended separately they reveal training needs, a confusing model, a new intake of residents, or a recent installation, and treated as failures they distort reliability data."},
{id:"u4l1-6",t:"u4l1",q:"A call arrives reporting that a device was in use when a patient was injured. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Repair the device quickly so the unit is not left short","Test the device and return it if it passes","Sequester the device with its accessories and settings undisturbed and notify risk management, because it is now evidence rather than a repair","Ask the unit to complete an incident form before you attend"],c:2,e:"A device involved in a patient event stops being a repair. Securing it with its accessories, disposables, and settings intact and notifying risk management preserves evidence that cannot be recovered once the device is serviced."},

// u4l2
{id:"u4l2-1",t:"u4l2",q:"A surveyor counts twenty-two ventilators on a unit while your inventory lists nineteen. The three extra arrived last month under a rental agreement. Why is this a finding?",a:["Rental equipment requires a separate agreement with the accreditor","Purchasing should not have signed a rental agreement","The inventory must cover every medical device in the building regardless of ownership and regardless of who maintains it","Rental devices must be purchased within thirty days"],c:2,e:"Ownership decides who pays, not who is accountable. Owned, leased, rented, borrowed, loaned, trial, and physician-owned devices all appear in the inventory, with who maintains each one and the evidence that it happened."},
{id:"u4l2-2",t:"u4l2",q:"A recall notice names a manufacturer, a model, and a range of serial numbers. Your inventory cannot produce a list of affected devices. Which missing field caused that?",a:["Location","Original cost","Acceptance date","Serial number"],c:3,e:"Serial ranges are what recalls name, which is why the rule is to inventory anything carrying a serial number and to record manufacturer, model, and serial in full. Without them a notice cannot be matched to real devices."},
{id:"u4l2-3",t:"u4l2",q:"You try to calculate the cost of service ratio by equipment type and find that half the assets return no result. Which field is almost certainly blank?",a:["Original cost, the price paid for the asset","Warranty end date","Risk score","Software version"],c:0,e:"The ratio is annual service cost divided by original acquisition cost, so an asset with no cost recorded cannot be included. Missing costs can be backfilled with an average of similar models, which is far closer than leaving zero."},
{id:"u4l2-4",t:"u4l2",q:"A surgeon brings in a device he owns personally and begins using it in his cases. What is the MOST appropriate action?",a:["Refuse to allow it in the building under any circumstances","Handle it as the equipment management plan requires: list it, record who maintains it, and hold the agreement and the maintenance evidence","Service it as though the hospital owned it","Report the device to the accrediting organization"],c:1,e:"The plan's inventory criteria must say how physician-owned, patient-owned, loaned, and trial equipment is handled. The device is listed, somebody is named as responsible for maintaining it, and the hospital holds evidence that maintenance happened."},
{id:"u4l2-5",t:"u4l2",q:"The hospital holds about one hundred intravenous poles, each worth very little and none carrying a serial number. What is the BEST way to handle them in the inventory?",a:["Create an individual asset record for each one","Leave them out entirely, since they are not medical devices","Give them one shared group record with a count, under a rule written into the equipment management plan","Record them in a spreadsheet kept by the supervisor"],c:2,e:"Numerous, inexpensive, low-consequence items may be covered by a single inventory line with a count. What is not acceptable is omitting them, because the question at survey is whether the hospital knows what it holds."},
{id:"u4l2-6",t:"u4l2",q:"Inventory accuracy has drifted and an annual count alone is not keeping up. What is the BEST way to improve it?",a:["Add a second annual count six months after the first","Freeze equipment movement between units","Ask each clinical department to submit its own equipment list","Check a sample of devices during routine rounds in every unit and record the asset numbers found in the system, alongside the annual count"],c:3,e:"Sampling across life support, critical, and support equipment during rounds finds more equipment per year than the annual sweep and produces something the sweep cannot: dated evidence that inventory accuracy is managed continuously."},

// u4l3
{id:"u4l3-1",t:"u4l3",q:"In one month 412 scheduled maintenance jobs were due, 371 were completed and documented, 18 were deferred with a reason, and 23 devices could not be located. What is the completion rate?",a:["94.4 percent","97.4 percent","85.4 percent","90.0 percent"],c:3,e:"Completion rate is completed divided by due times 100, so 371 / 412 x 100 is 90.0 percent. Neither deferred work nor devices that could not be located counts as complete."},
{id:"u4l3-2",t:"u4l3",q:"Twenty-three devices due for scheduled maintenance could not be found anywhere on their units. How should they be treated in the monthly numbers?",a:["As not complete, and as evidence of an inventory and access problem rather than a maintenance problem","As excluded from the denominator, since they were unavailable","As completed, since the department made a reasonable effort","As deferred, with no further action required"],c:0,e:"Could-not-locate devices are not complete, and the cause is usually inaccurate locations or no access rather than a shortage of technicians. They need a written search procedure, notification, escalation, and an accounting decision."},
{id:"u4l3-3",t:"u4l3",q:"It is the twenty-eighth of the month and completion stands at 71 percent with eight days before the committee report. What management failure does this reveal?",a:["Too few technicians are employed","Completion was being monitored at month end rather than daily, so the month was lost before anyone could act","The schedule should be quarterly rather than monthly","The committee report date is set too early"],c:1,e:"Scheduled work is planned and tracked monthly, so a figure read only at month end tells a manager nothing in time to fix anything. Monitored daily, a technician falling behind can be given help or a changed workload."},
{id:"u4l3-4",t:"u4l3",q:"An annual scheduled inspection was due on March 10 and was performed on April 2. Is the hospital compliant?",a:["No, because annual work must fall in the month it was scheduled","No, because any late work must be reported as a miss","Yes, because annual scheduled maintenance carries a window of 30 days either side of its due date","Yes, because annual work may be done at any time in the calendar year"],c:2,e:"The accreditor allows 30 days before or after the scheduled date for annual scheduled maintenance, which is what makes a monthly plan workable. Work inside that window is complete, not late."},
{id:"u4l3-5",t:"u4l3",q:"Besides the completion rate itself, what MUST the monthly compliance report contain?",a:["The name of the technician responsible for each job","The cost of the month's scheduled maintenance","A comparison against the published national average","The rate by department, the high-risk and life-support subset, and the list of misses with a reason and a closing date for each"],c:3,e:"A documented miss is a finding you can answer and an undocumented one is not. The report carries the rate overall and by department, the critical subset reported separately, every miss with its reason, and the date each was closed."},
{id:"u4l3-6",t:"u4l3",q:"Outlying clinics came in at 59 percent completion, with twelve of eighteen misses caused by devices nobody could find. What is the BEST corrective action?",a:["Audit the clinic inventory and agree a standing access window with the clinic managers, since this is an inventory and access failure","Schedule the clinics twice a year instead of once","Add a technician to cover the clinics","Remove the clinics from the scheduled maintenance program"],c:0,e:"Name the cause before choosing the fix. Devices that cannot be located are an inventory accuracy and access problem, and more labor applied to inaccurate records produces the same result at higher cost."},

// u4l4
{id:"u4l4-1",t:"u4l4",q:"Leadership wants one measure that reflects what a clinician actually experiences when a device fails. Which is the MOST appropriate to report?",a:["Total time to repair, from the call until the device is back in service","Mean time to repair, the average hands-on repair time","Work orders closed per technician","The scheduled maintenance completion rate"],c:0,e:"Total time to repair includes waiting for parts, vendors, and access to the room, which is the whole wait the unit feels. Mean time to repair measures only the technician and the parts supply and is always the shorter number."},
{id:"u4l4-2",t:"u4l4",q:"Open work orders have risen from 70 to 118 and the oldest item has aged from 22 days to 54. What does this pattern represent?",a:["A documentation problem, since old orders are usually already finished","The earliest honest warning that capacity, parts supply, or vendor performance has broken down","Normal seasonal variation that needs no action","Evidence that triage is working correctly"],c:1,e:"Backlog reported with age is the first measure to move when a department is losing ground, which is why it belongs on the short set leadership sees alongside completion, repair time, satisfaction, and cost."},
{id:"u4l4-3",t:"u4l4",q:"A peer hospital reports 310 labor hours per hundred devices against your 190, and your director wants an explanation. What is the BEST response?",a:["Accept the comparison and investigate why your department is faster","Ask the peer hospital for its raw data so the comparison can be corrected","Explain that labor hours per device is only valid inside one hospital, because the figure changes entirely with how each inventory was counted","Stop reporting the measure"],c:2,e:"A department that inventories every module looks twice as productive as one that counts whole systems, and scope differences in imaging, laboratory, and non-clinical equipment compound it. The measure is useful for your own trend and misleading between organizations."},
{id:"u4l4-4",t:"u4l4",q:"Eleven repairs returned within thirty days of the original fix, and nine of them were on one pump model. What does that cluster MOST likely indicate?",a:["A training need for the technician who closed them","That the repairs were documented incorrectly","That the thirty-day definition is too generous","A problem with that model, a bad batch of parts, or a technique issue specific to it, which a vendor advisory request can help settle"],c:3,e:"A repeat repair is one that recurs within 30 days and can mean a troublesome device, a bad part, or a training need. When nine of eleven land on a single model, the device or its parts supply is the first hypothesis, not the technician."},
{id:"u4l4-5",t:"u4l4",q:"Your documentation rate has settled at 81 percent of paid hours and a director asks why it is not 100 percent. What is the BEST answer?",a:["Around 85 percent is the realistic ceiling once breaks, the gaps between jobs, and general shop activity are accounted for, and chasing 100 percent produces fiction","The system is losing entries and needs replacing","Technicians are not working the full day","One hundred percent is achievable and should be the target"],c:0,e:"The aim is to document every paid hour against something, with as much as possible against a tagged asset, then a clinical department, then the department itself. Everyone loses about an hour a day, so about 85 percent is the honest maximum."},
{id:"u4l4-6",t:"u4l4",q:"You intend to claim a $35,000 annual saving from insourcing one fleet. What must the report contain for the claim to survive finance review?",a:["The vendor's original quotation and your purchase orders","The baseline from your own records, the change and its date, the actual cost over the same number of months including loaded in-house labor, and what else changed in the period","A letter from the clinical department confirming satisfaction","A comparison against the published cost of service band"],c:1,e:"A saving that omits in-house labor at its loaded rate will be dismantled by the first analyst who looks at it. Baseline, change, actual over the same period, and the other variables are what make the number defensible."},

// u4l5
{id:"u4l5-1",t:"u4l5",q:"A device cost $400,000 and the hospital spends $44,000 a year to keep it running. What is its cost of service ratio?",a:["9.1 percent","11.0 percent","4.4 percent","1.1 percent"],c:1,e:"The ratio is annual service cost divided by original acquisition cost times 100, so 44,000 / 400,000 x 100 is 11 percent. Both numbers should already be in the inventory and the work order history."},
{id:"u4l5-2",t:"u4l5",q:"A comparison of service options shows the in-house option costing almost nothing per year. What has MOST likely been left out?",a:["Vendor travel charges","Depreciation on the device","The department's own labor at its fully loaded rate","The cost of raising purchase orders"],c:2,e:"Annual service cost means contracts, parts, outside labor, loaded in-house labor, and allocated overhead. Omitting your own labor makes every in-house option look free and is the most common way this calculation gets rigged by accident."},
{id:"u4l5-3",t:"u4l5",q:"Your program sits at a respectable 6 percent overall and administration has asked for cost reduction. Where should you look FIRST?",a:["At the aggregate figure over the last five years","At the total labor hours recorded last year","At the published benchmark band for comparable hospitals","At the drill-down by hospital, department, modality, equipment type, and manufacturer"],c:3,e:"A healthy average easily hides one modality at triple the norm and one manufacturer whose parts pricing carries its own margin. Sort the drill-down by ratio and work from the top; the aggregate exists to be reported, not to find money."},
{id:"u4l5-4",t:"u4l5",q:"Which cost of service ratio range would you expect from equipment maintained entirely in-house on suitable models?",a:["4 to 6 percent","10 to 25 percent","25 to 40 percent","Under 1 percent"],c:0,e:"Manufacturer-maintained equipment typically runs 10 to 25 percent whether under contract or on time and materials, while fully in-house work on suitable equipment can fall as low as 4 to 6 percent."},
{id:"u4l5-5",t:"u4l5",q:"A ventilator fleet returns an unusually low cost of service ratio of 0.9 percent. What should you check before celebrating?",a:["Whether the acquisition costs were entered too high","Whether the scheduled maintenance is actually being performed and documented","Whether the fleet is still under warranty from a previous year","Whether the devices are counted individually or as systems"],c:1,e:"Low is not automatically good. An unusually low ratio on critical equipment often means scheduled work is not being done or hours are not being documented against the asset, both of which are worse problems than a high ratio."},
{id:"u4l5-6",t:"u4l5",q:"A consultant presents a published cost of service band and concludes your program is inefficient. What is the BEST reply?",a:["Accept the conclusion and set the band as the department target","Argue that the ratio is not a valid measure of an equipment program","Point out that programs differ in what they count as service cost, how they inventory, whether imaging and laboratory work is included, and how overhead is allocated, so your own tracked history is the defensible comparison","Request the consultant recalculate using devices per technician instead"],c:2,e:"Dividing by acquisition cost lets a thermometer and a scanner be ranked on one scale, which is why the measure travels well inside an organization. Across organizations the definitions differ, so a published band is a prompt rather than a verdict."},

// u4l6
{id:"u4l6-1",t:"u4l6",q:"Your workload build totals 6,782 annual hours and a position delivers 1,560 productive hours after leave, holidays, training, and daily lost time. How many positions does the work require?",a:["3.3","5.2","4.3","2.9"],c:2,e:"6,782 divided by 1,560 is 4.3 positions. Dividing by the 2,080 paid hours in a full-time position instead would have returned 3.3 and understated the requirement by a whole person."},
{id:"u4l6-2",t:"u4l6",q:"A colleague divides total workload hours by 2,080 to arrive at a staffing number. What does that produce?",a:["A correct figure, since one full-time position is 2,080 paid hours a year","An overstatement, because 2,080 excludes overtime","The same answer as dividing by productive hours","An understatement, because leave, holidays, training, and daily lost time mean a position delivers fewer productive hours than it is paid for"],c:3,e:"One full-time equivalent is about 2,080 paid hours, but the divisor in a staffing calculation is the productive hours a real person delivers. Using paid hours quietly understates the requirement, and administration will not correct it for you."},
{id:"u4l6-3",t:"u4l6",q:"Your three technicians support 1,651 inventoried items, which falls inside the historic band of 1,200 to 2,000 items per technician, yet overtime is 80 percent above plan. How should that band be used?",a:["As a sanity check only, because it ignores complexity, what is on contract, and how the inventory was counted","As the primary basis for the staffing request, since it is an accepted industry figure","As evidence that the department is correctly staffed and the overtime is a scheduling problem","As a reason to re-inventory the hospital at module level"],c:0,e:"The device-count figure reports a department as correctly sized while completion slips and overtime runs high. Build the answer from hours and use the band only to sanity-check it, remembering it is hostage to how the inventory was counted."},
{id:"u4l6-4",t:"u4l6",q:"There is a hiring freeze and your scheduled maintenance backlog justifies another position. What argument is MOST likely to succeed?",a:["The monthly scheduled maintenance workload, presented in hours","The cost avoided by declining a vendor contract on a system coming out of warranty, with the position costing less than the contract","The risk of a regulatory finding if completion rates keep falling","A comparison with staffing levels at peer hospitals"],c:1,e:"Work that prevents problems is invisible to the people who approve headcount, so requests built on preventive workload rarely succeed. A position funded by a contract the hospital no longer signs is a financial argument administration recognizes."},
{id:"u4l6-5",t:"u4l6",q:"Only one technician is trained on a life-support model the hospital owns nine of. What is the BEST recommendation?",a:["Place the model on a full service contract","Cross-train the whole department on the model","Fund a second training seat, because one trained technician is a single point of failure the first time they take leave","Accept the risk and arrange vendor cover when needed"],c:2,e:"Parts access and a trained technician favor in-house work, but a single seat is not a program. Every critical skill needs a second person funded so leave, sickness, and turnover do not take the capability out with them."},
{id:"u4l6-6",t:"u4l6",q:"Evening and weekend call volume has been steady for eight months and overtime has run above plan throughout. What is the BEST structural answer?",a:["Keep the overtime, since it is cheaper than a position","Add an on-call allowance for the evening hours","Move the scheduled maintenance program to weekends","Introduce a second shift that covers the hours clinicians actually work"],c:3,e:"Cover the hours clinicians work rather than the hours the shop prefers. Steady evening and weekend volume is answered by a shift, and sustained overtime over months is a position in disguise."},

// u4l7
{id:"u4l7-1",t:"u4l7",q:"An instrument is returned from calibration marked out of tolerance. What must your written procedure require FIRST?",a:["Replace the instrument and continue the month's work","Recalibrate the instrument and return it to service","Notify the accrediting organization of the lapse","Quarantine the instrument and identify every record it produced since its last good certificate, then assess the clinical risk"],c:3,e:"A reading taken with an out-of-tolerance instrument casts doubt on the record it produced, which can mean re-testing a fleet. The procedure quarantines the instrument, identifies the affected records, assesses risk, re-tests what must be re-tested, and reports."},
{id:"u4l7-2",t:"u4l7",q:"A technician wants to use a flow analyzer the device manufacturer does not name in its procedure. Is that acceptable?",a:["Yes, provided the instrument gives results equivalent to what the manufacturer's procedure requires and is maintained so its readings are reliable","No, only the manufacturer's named instrument may be used","Yes, but only when the manufacturer's instrument is unavailable","No, unless the manufacturer gives written permission"],c:0,e:"Tools used for maintenance need not be those the manufacturer specifically recommends, but they must be capable of producing equivalent results and must be maintained so measurements can be relied on."},
{id:"u4l7-3",t:"u4l7",q:"Six years after purchase, the department still has no service manual or diagnostic password for a model it maintains. When was the BEST moment to secure them?",a:["At the first warranty renewal","Before signature, written into the purchase agreement as named deliverables","At the first failure, when the need could be demonstrated","Whenever the manufacturer publishes an updated manual"],c:1,e:"Nothing obliges a manufacturer to supply manuals, software, keys, or training, and after the purchase order is signed the leverage is gone. Each item is named as a deliverable, with payment withheld until it actually arrives."},
{id:"u4l7-4",t:"u4l7",q:"Three scheduled jobs could not be finished because the preventive maintenance kits had not been ordered. Where does that failure become visible, and how is it prevented?",a:["In the parts budget, prevented by holding more stock of everything","In the satisfaction survey, prevented by telling the unit","In the monthly compliance report as missed scheduled maintenance, prevented by ordering kits and supplies in the month before they are needed","In the overtime figures, prevented by weekend working"],c:2,e:"A missing resource never stays a resource problem. It appears as a miss on the compliance report, and the standing control is to generate the coming month's work in advance and order kits and supplies before the month begins."},
{id:"u4l7-5",t:"u4l7",q:"A new technician asks what personal protective equipment is required for work in a sterile processing area. What determines the answer?",a:["The technician's own judgment on the day","The manufacturer's service manual for the device being serviced","The equipment the previous technician used","The hazard assessment for that area, with the equipment issued and the training recorded"],c:3,e:"Required protection follows the hazard assessment for each area, and the management duty is to issue it, train on it, record the training, and inspect it. The record is the part that fails an audit, not the glove box."},
{id:"u4l7-6",t:"u4l7",q:"The department needs a new safety analyzer priced above the hospital's capitalization threshold. Where does the request belong?",a:["In the capital budget, where it competes with every other capital request in the hospital","In the operating budget, under supplies","In the maintenance and repairs account","In the travel and education account"],c:0,e:"Major test equipment clears the capitalization threshold and has a long useful life, so it is a capital request competing hospital-wide. Small instruments, kits, consumables, and calibration sit in the operating budget."},

// u4l8
{id:"u4l8-1",t:"u4l8",q:"The work order system was restored from a backup nobody had ever tested, and eleven days of records were lost. What is the MOST appropriate preventive action?",a:["Put a scheduled restore test in the calendar, perform it, and document the result for every system you depend on","Move the backup to a different storage provider","Increase the backup frequency to twice daily","Ask the vendor to confirm in writing that backups are running"],c:0,e:"A backup nobody has restored is a hope rather than a plan. Each system needs a frequency, a copy held away from the machine it protects, an allowed restore time, an acceptable data loss, and a documented restore test."},
{id:"u4l8-2",t:"u4l8",q:"A monitoring central station was rebuilt from its weekly backup and came back without the unit's user list. What would have caught this in advance?",a:["A second backup copy held off site","A restore test, which is the only thing that proves what a backup actually contains","Backing up more frequently than weekly","A written agreement with the manufacturer"],c:1,e:"The backup had been running successfully for months and did not contain what anyone assumed. Only a real restore reveals that, which is why the restore test and its documented result is the control rather than the backup schedule."},
{id:"u4l8-3",t:"u4l8",q:"The shop uses one shared login for the work order system because it is quicker. What is the central objection?",a:["It breaches the software license","It makes password changes more difficult","It destroys the audit trail, so no record can be attributed to a person when an investigation needs it","It slows the system down"],c:2,e:"Named individual accounts with role-based rights are the basis of every later investigation, competency question, and access review. A shared login removes attribution from every record created through it."},
{id:"u4l8-4",t:"u4l8",q:"A manufacturer states that a security patch is not supported on its device and will void the configuration. What is the BEST response?",a:["Apply the patch anyway, because security takes priority","Leave the device unpatched and take no further action","Remove the device from the network permanently","Apply compensating controls such as segmentation, restricted access, and monitoring, and record the decision with the security officer's agreement"],c:3,e:"A device is cleared in a configuration, so an unvalidated change can take it outside that configuration. The defensible position is a documented compensating control agreed with the security officer, not an unremarked gap."},
{id:"u4l8-5",t:"u4l8",q:"A configuration change made quietly on a group of clinical devices caused an outage, and nobody could restore the previous state. Which two elements of change control were missing?",a:["A back-out plan and a version record against each asset","Approval and notification","A risk assessment and a test","A scheduled window and a vendor contact"],c:0,e:"The pattern behind nearly every self-inflicted clinical outage is the same: nobody could say afterward what had changed, so nobody could put it back. The back-out plan and the recorded version are what prevent it."},
{id:"u4l8-6",t:"u4l8",q:"An audit finds live accounts belonging to two people who left the department last year and a vendor account from an expired contract. What control would have prevented both?",a:["Longer password complexity requirements","Account closure built into the exit checklist and revocation at contract end, backed by a signed periodic access review","Two-person approval for new accounts","Annual security awareness training"],c:1,e:"These two findings recur in every department that has not run the review as a signed list. Accounts close on the day somebody leaves, vendor access is revoked when the contract ends, and a periodic review catches what slipped."},

// u4l9
{id:"u4l9-1",t:"u4l9",q:"A review finds four diagnostic imaging units maintained on intervals the department set rather than the manufacturer's. What is the problem?",a:["The intervals are longer than the manufacturer recommends","Imaging and radiologic equipment, whether diagnostic or therapeutic, is not eligible for an alternative maintenance program at all","The risk assessment was not reviewed this year","The decision was made by a technician rather than an engineer"],c:1,e:"Imaging and radiologic equipment used for diagnostic or therapeutic purposes must be maintained according to the manufacturer's recommendations. No risk assessment, however thorough, makes it eligible."},
{id:"u4l9-2",t:"u4l9",q:"A new ventilator model has just arrived and the risk profile suggests a longer interval than the manufacturer recommends. May it enter the alternative program now?",a:["Yes, if a qualified person documents the risk assessment","Yes, because ventilators are not on the excluded list","No. New equipment stays on the manufacturer's recommendations until enough maintenance history exists to support the change","No, because life-support equipment can never be included"],c:2,e:"New equipment without sufficient maintenance history is excluded. History may come from the hospital, its contractor, or publicly available nationally recognized sources, and a later move requires evidence that the track record and risk were evaluated and the alternative regimen tested."},
{id:"u4l9-3",t:"u4l9",q:"Who may decide that a piece of medical equipment can safely be maintained on intervals other than the manufacturer's?",a:["The hospital's safety officer","The environment of care committee by vote","The manufacturer's service representative","Qualified personnel such as a clinical or biomedical technician or engineer, whether employee or contractor, with qualification records held"],c:3,e:"The determination must be made by qualified personnel regardless of whether they are employees or contractors, highly specialized equipment may need specialist knowledge, and the hospital keeps records of those qualifications and shows how contractors' people are assured."},
{id:"u4l9-4",t:"u4l9",q:"The hospital set an annual interval for a device type in its alternative program, and technicians have been performing the work whenever they are in the area, sometimes at eighteen months. What is the violation?",a:["A hospital must adhere strictly to the activities and frequencies it established for its own program","The interval exceeds what the manufacturer recommends","The work is not being documented against the asset","Annual intervals are not permitted for devices in the program"],c:0,e:"Regulators permit the change in frequency but not improvisation. Once the hospital sets its own activities and frequencies it must follow them strictly, and a program that drifts is worse than having none."},
{id:"u4l9-5",t:"u4l9",q:"Which combination of evidence is required to support placing an equipment type in an alternative maintenance program?",a:["The manufacturer's written consent and the clinical department's agreement","Function and the consequences of failure, how serious and widespread the harm would be, maintenance requirements, availability of backup devices, and documented incident history of identical or very similar equipment","Three years of completion rates and the current cost of service ratio","A benchmarking study against comparable hospitals"],c:1,e:"Several factors are weighed together because severity and likelihood combine differently for different equipment, and the same model may carry different risk in a different care setting."},
{id:"u4l9-6",t:"u4l9",q:"A surveyor begins reviewing your alternative maintenance program. Which equipment will they concentrate on?",a:["The oldest equipment in the program","The equipment with the longest intervals","Critical equipment inside the program, and the documentation of the factors and evidence behind its strategy","A random sample across all equipment types"],c:2,e:"Surveyors are directed to focus on critical equipment within the program, which is biomedical or physical plant equipment whose failure risks serious injury or death. Critical equipment must be readily identifiable whether it is in the program or not."},

// u5l1
{id:"u5l1-1",t:"u5l1",q:"A new director asks what kind of document the medical equipment management plan is. What is the correct description?",a:["A departmental procedure manual for the technical staff","A contract between the department and the clinical departments","An organization-wide document stating the hospital's approach to maintaining its medical devices, specific enough to show how each equipment type is maintained and demonstrated","An annual report of maintenance performance"],c:2,e:"The plan is organization-wide rather than departmental, written to stand up against federal, state, and local requirements, and specific enough that a reader can see how each equipment type is maintained and how that maintenance is proved."},
{id:"u5l1-2",t:"u5l1",q:"Which section of the plan does a surveyor read when they want to know who is accountable for what?",a:["The performance monitoring section","The compliance statement","The inventory inclusion criteria","The responsibilities section, which spells out the day-to-day duties of the department, clinical staff, contractors, and manufacturers"],c:3,e:"The plan has to state day-to-day duties explicitly across clinicians, in-house staff, and contract providers. Vagueness there is a finding on its own, because the next question is always where it says so."},
{id:"u5l1-3",t:"u5l1",q:"Your plan repeats the text of the hospital's purchasing policy and its information security policy at length. What is the drawback?",a:["The copies fall out of step with the originals, which is why a plan references the organization's other policies rather than repeating them","The plan becomes too long for a surveyor to read","Purchasing will object to the duplication","The plan then requires approval from two committees"],c:0,e:"Pointing from the plan to the organization's other policies is the correct approach. Duplicated text is a second version that nobody updates, and two versions of a policy means the hospital cannot say which one governed the work."},
{id:"u5l1-4",t:"u5l1",q:"What is the purpose of the plan's annual evaluation, and who approves it?",a:["A full rewrite of the plan, approved by senior leadership","A short written review of scope, objectives, performance, and effectiveness, presented to and approved by the environment of care committee","An internal audit of work order records, approved by the department director","A benchmarking exercise, approved by the accrediting organization"],c:1,e:"The evaluation answers four questions: was the scope right, were the objectives met, what do the numbers say, and was the program effective. Approval is recorded in the committee minutes, which is the evidence that leadership accepted it."},
{id:"u5l1-5",t:"u5l1",q:"The department changes its maintenance strategy for a group of equipment types next month. When should the plan be updated?",a:["At the next annual evaluation, with the change noted","Only if a surveyor asks about that equipment type","Before the first device moves to the new interval, because the plan has to describe the program the hospital is actually running","After six months of data confirms the new strategy works"],c:2,e:"The hospital is judged against what the plan says rather than what the department does. An out-of-date strategy section means either the plan is wrong or the practice is unauthorized, and a surveyor will choose the less favorable reading."},
{id:"u5l1-6",t:"u5l1",q:"Where does the medical equipment management plan sit within the hospital's wider documentation?",a:["Inside the purchasing policy manual","Inside the human resources policy library","Inside the quality management system as a standalone chapter","Inside the environment of care program, alongside the plans for safety, security, hazardous materials, fire safety, and utilities"],c:3,e:"The equipment plan is one of the environment of care plans, which is why devices on the emergency branch appear in both it and the utilities plan, and why construction touches the equipment, fire safety, and infection control programs together."},

// u5l2
{id:"u5l2-1",t:"u5l2",q:"A technician correctly describes what the department does when a device due for maintenance cannot be found, but no written procedure exists. Why is this still a finding?",a:["Because the practice described was not the correct one","Because the practice was not approved by the committee","Because only managers may answer that question","Because an unwritten practice cannot be audited, taught, or proved, and a surveyor is entitled to disbelieve it"],c:3,e:"Procedures are the bridge between the plan and the records: the plan says what the hospital does, the procedure says how, and the record says it happened. A missing procedure breaks the chain however good the practice is."},
{id:"u5l2-2",t:"u5l2",q:"Which element of a standard operating procedure is MOST often written incorrectly in a way that causes problems later?",a:["Naming individuals rather than roles in the responsibility section","Listing definitions of technical terms","Numbering the steps","Recording the approval date"],c:0,e:"Responsibility is assigned by role, never by name, because people change and documents should not have to. A procedure naming individuals becomes wrong the first time somebody transfers or leaves."},
{id:"u5l2-3",t:"u5l2",q:"Three versions of the same procedure are in circulation in different folders. What does that mean in practice?",a:["Staff can choose whichever version suits the situation","None of them governs, because you cannot prove which version applied to work performed in a given month","Only the newest version counts, so the others are harmless","The procedure must be rewritten from scratch"],c:1,e:"Document control means one source, a version and effective date, a named owner, a review cycle, a change record, and superseded versions withdrawn. A procedure that exists in three versions effectively exists in none."},
{id:"u5l2-4",t:"u5l2",q:"A procedure has just been revised and the new version takes effect next week. What else must happen?",a:["The old version is archived and the change recorded, and that is sufficient","The committee must re-approve the whole procedure library","Affected staff are retrained and the training recorded against the new version and date","Each technician signs a statement that they have read it"],c:2,e:"Training against a procedure is what converts a document into a practice, and the dated training record is what lets you prove it. This is the half most departments skip, and the half an auditor asks for."},
{id:"u5l2-5",t:"u5l2",q:"Which document is the organization's binder of plans that holds the equipment plan beside safety, security, hazardous materials, fire, and utilities?",a:["The accreditation evidence binder","The quality management system manual","The department policy and procedure manual","The environment of care manual"],c:3,e:"The environment of care manual is where a surveyor reaches your content. Know where it lives, which version is current, and which of its pages your department is accountable for."},
{id:"u5l2-6",t:"u5l2",q:"A surveyor asks whether your procedure library is managed rather than merely present. Which single document answers that BEST?",a:["The document control register listing each procedure with its version, effective date, owner, and review date","The equipment management plan","The training matrix for technical staff","The list of procedures referenced in the plan"],c:0,e:"The register answers four questions at once: what exists, which version governs, who owns it, and whether the review is current. It takes about an hour a year to maintain."},

// u5l3
{id:"u5l3-1",t:"u5l3",q:"Which of these is written as an objective rather than as a goal?",a:["Total time to repair on critical equipment reduced from 3.8 days to 3.0 days by the end of the fiscal year, owned by the supervisor","Be more responsive to clinical departments","Improve scheduled maintenance compliance","Reduce the department cost of service"],c:0,e:"A goal is a direction and an objective is a goal with a number, a date, and an owner. Written that way, failure is visible, which is the point."},
{id:"u5l3-2",t:"u5l3",q:"A device-related near miss involving a confusing alarm configuration has been investigated. Which committee is the MOST appropriate place to take the trend and the proposed practice change?",a:["The capital or technology committee","The patient safety committee","The performance improvement committee","The radiation safety committee"],c:1,e:"Patient safety receives device-related events and near misses, use error trends, and alarm issues, and it can mandate a clinical practice change that no amount of equipment work would achieve."},
{id:"u5l3-3",t:"u5l3",q:"Infection control has asked what your department brings to its meetings. What belongs on that report?",a:["Scheduled maintenance completion rates by department","The capital plan for the coming year","Device cleaning and disinfection between patients and before service, water systems, sterilizers, and endoscope reprocessing equipment","Technician competency assessment results"],c:2,e:"Each committee gets the measure it is responsible for. Infection control's equipment interest is cleaning and disinfection, water systems, sterilizers, reprocessing equipment, and the handling of loaner and rental devices arriving from outside."},
{id:"u5l3-4",t:"u5l3",q:"What is the MOST effective structure for a monthly report to a committee?",a:["A full data set with the department's commentary appended","A comparison of your metrics against peer hospitals","A narrative summary of the month's activity","The number, the trend, the cause, the action, and what you need from the committee, on one page"],c:3,e:"A committee reads the first two lines and then decides whether to read the rest. Putting the cause before the action stops the report becoming a list of activity with no diagnosis, and the ask is what turns an audience into a decision."},
{id:"u5l3-5",t:"u5l3",q:"A committee agreed verbally to a standing quarterly access window in the outlying clinics, but the minutes do not mention it. What follows?",a:["The commitment effectively did not happen, because the minutes are the record and the later survey evidence","The agreement stands, since it was reached in the meeting","The chair can confirm it by email after the fact without further action","The agreement must be re-negotiated with each clinic manager individually"],c:0,e:"A decision that is not minuted did not happen, and next year's evidence is assembled from those minutes. Confirming the decision in the minutes is part of presenting to a committee."},
{id:"u5l3-6",t:"u5l3",q:"You are on six committee calendars and reports are regularly late. What is the MOST appropriate first step?",a:["Ask to be removed from the committees that read your report least","Build and publish a reporting calendar naming each body, its meeting date, what is due, who prepares it, and when the data closes","Send the same report to every committee to save preparation time","Report quarterly to all of them instead of monthly"],c:1,e:"A report that misses a meeting waits a month, and a number that waits a month stops being news. The calendar is built once a year and published where the supervisors who produce the data can see it."},

// u5l4
{id:"u5l4-1",t:"u5l4",q:"Why does an equipment maintenance finding attract attention from executives who have never visited your shop?",a:["Because equipment findings carry statutory fines","Because the federal conditions of participation are tied to payment for most of the hospital's patients, so a finding threatens participation rather than producing a fine","Because accreditation surveys are published publicly","Because the finding must be reported to the device agency"],c:1,e:"The agency that pays for a large share of hospital care sets the conditions a hospital must meet to be paid, and several of them reach equipment maintenance and documentation directly. The exposure is participation, not a penalty."},
{id:"u5l4-2",t:"u5l4",q:"A hospital is accredited by an approved accrediting organization. What does deemed status mean for it?",a:["The hospital is exempt from state licensure requirements","The accrediting organization assumes legal liability for compliance","The hospital is deemed to meet the federal conditions of participation, and the accreditor's survey substitutes for the routine federal one","The hospital may set its own equipment maintenance standards"],c:2,e:"The federal agency approves accrediting organizations to survey on its behalf, and their standards must be at least as demanding as the federal requirements. The agency retains the right to validate that work and to investigate complaints."},
{id:"u5l4-3",t:"u5l4",q:"Your risk scoring would place a laboratory instrument on a longer interval, but the laboratory's accreditation checklist sets a specific frequency for it. Which governs?",a:["The hospital's risk-based assessment, since it is the more recent analysis","The interval the environment of care committee approves","Whichever interval the manufacturer recommends","The laboratory checklist frequency, because a stricter service-specific requirement overrides your own scoring"],c:3,e:"Compliance is a stack and the stricter layer always wins. Laboratory checklists, blood bank standards, and state rules can all set frequencies that limit how far a hospital may adjust its own intervals."},
{id:"u5l4-4",t:"u5l4",q:"Which body surveys hospitals that are not accredited, performs validation surveys, and investigates complaints at any hospital?",a:["The state survey agency","The device agency's device center","The accrediting organization that holds deeming authority in that state","The state hospital association"],c:0,e:"The state survey agency acts on the federal agency's behalf for non-accredited hospitals, carries out validation work, and investigates complaints regardless of a hospital's accreditation status."},
{id:"u5l4-5",t:"u5l4",q:"Which requirement applies to equipment before it is used for the first time and again after a major repair or upgrade?",a:["Registration with the device agency","Inspection and testing for performance and safety, with the record retained","A risk assessment by qualified personnel","Approval by the environment of care committee"],c:1,e:"All equipment must be inspected and tested for performance and safety before initial use and after major repairs or upgrades, which is why the incoming inspection and the post-repair verification records are standard survey evidence."},
{id:"u5l4-6",t:"u5l4",q:"A manager proposes performing maintenance twice as often as the manufacturer recommends, to be safe. Is that permitted, and on what condition?",a:["No, any deviation from the manufacturer's schedule requires an alternative program","Yes, and no documentation of the manufacturer's recommendations is then required","Yes, a hospital may maintain equipment more frequently than recommended, provided it still performs the manufacturer's recommended activities","No, because more frequent intervention increases risk"],c:2,e:"Following the manufacturer is always compliant, and exceeding the frequency is allowed as long as the manufacturer's activities are the ones performed. The documentation of their recommendations and your matching records still has to be held."},

// u5l5
{id:"u5l5-1",t:"u5l5",q:"A vendor engineer will take a device containing patient data to a repair depot. What must be true before it leaves the building?",a:["The device is tagged as out of service in the inventory","The clinical department approves the shipment in writing","The media is wiped or removed, or a signed business associate agreement covers the vendor's handling of the data","The device agency is notified of the removal"],c:2,e:"The security rule reaches every device that creates, receives, maintains, or transmits electronic patient data. Often only the equipment department knows the data is there, which makes the wipe or the agreement a standing duty on every route out."},
{id:"u5l5-2",t:"u5l5",q:"A vendor arrives to install a wireless system with access points of its own. How should that be treated?",a:["As a routine installation, since the vendor supplies the equipment","As a construction project requiring an infection control risk assessment","As an information technology matter with no equipment department involvement","As a spectrum coordination request before it is an installation, checked against the hospital's telemetry use and transmitter register"],c:3,e:"Protected telemetry spectrum exists so monitored waveforms are not competing with everything else in the building. Someone must register the hospital's use, hold a frequency and transmitter record, and keep other equipment out of the band."},
{id:"u5l5-3",t:"u5l5",q:"Nuclear medicine asks you to service a camera that handles radioactive sources. What is the correct boundary?",a:["Do no work until the radiation safety officer has cleared it, because the license is held by the hospital and administered through the radiation safety program","Proceed, since the device is on your inventory","Refer the work to the manufacturer in all cases","Obtain written permission from the regulator before each job"],c:0,e:"Licensing and inspection of radioactive material use sits with the federal commission, exercised in much of the country by a state under agreement with it. The license belongs to the hospital and is administered by the radiation safety officer and committee."},
{id:"u5l5-4",t:"u5l5",q:"Which duty does the device reporting regulation place on the hospital as a device user facility, beyond submitting reports?",a:["Registering the hospital's device inventory annually","Developing, maintaining, and implementing written reporting procedures, and establishing event files available to an authorized agency employee","Obtaining the manufacturer's agreement before filing","Reporting every device malfunction regardless of outcome"],c:1,e:"The written procedures cover identifying, evaluating, and transmitting reportable events, and the event file holds what was evaluated, the deliberations about reportability, copies of submissions, and the agency's acknowledgments."},
{id:"u5l5-5",t:"u5l5",q:"The hospital is considering buying a refurbished device from a third-party company. Which regulatory question is MOST worth asking?",a:["Whether the company is accredited by a hospital accrediting organization","Whether the company holds a radioactive materials license","Whether the rebuild significantly changed the device's performance or safety specifications or its intended use, which would make the company a remanufacturer under the agency's quality system requirements","Whether the company is registered with the spectrum agency"],c:2,e:"A person who processes, conditions, renovates, repackages, or restores a finished device so that its performance or safety specifications or its intended use are significantly changed is a remanufacturer, and that counts as a manufacturer under the quality system requirements. Rebuilding that changes none of the three is servicing, so ask what was actually changed."},
{id:"u5l5-6",t:"u5l5",q:"A laptop holding test results from forty patients has gone missing from the shop. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Search the department thoroughly before raising it, to avoid a false alarm","Wipe the remaining shop computers as a precaution","Report the loss to the device agency","Notify the privacy officer immediately, because breach response runs on its own clock and belongs to that role"],c:3,e:"Suspected loss or exposure of patient information goes to the privacy officer at once. Breach response has its own timetable and its own owner, and delay while the department searches is itself a problem."},

// u5l6
{id:"u5l6-1",t:"u5l6",q:"Two surveyors arrive on a Tuesday morning with no notice. What determines how the week goes?",a:["How quickly the evidence binder can be assembled that morning","Whether the department director is available to host","Which surveyor is assigned to the environment of care","Whether the evidence has been kept current year-round, so producing it is retrieval rather than a project"],c:3,e:"Accreditation surveys are unannounced and the federal agency's own surveyors may arrive to validate or to investigate a complaint. Preparation cannot be an event, so the only workable approach is current evidence with a known location and owner per item."},
{id:"u5l6-2",t:"u5l6",q:"A surveyor reads the label on an infusion pump in a critical care unit and begins asking about that specific device. What method is this?",a:["A tracer, which follows one real device into the inventory, the schedule, the service history, the competency record of whoever serviced it, and the plan","A document review","A validation survey","An equipment inventory audit"],c:0,e:"A tracer starts at the bedside rather than in your binder, and weak links appear immediately: a tag not in the inventory, a history with a gap, or an interval nobody can explain. Running your own tracers monthly finds them first."},
{id:"u5l6-3",t:"u5l6",q:"A surveyor asks a question you cannot answer from memory. What is the BEST response?",a:["Give your best estimate, since hesitating looks evasive","Say you will find out, then find out and come back with the record","Explain that the question is outside your department's scope","Refer the surveyor to the department director"],c:1,e:"Answer the question that was asked, show the record, and let the record speak. Do not speculate, do not guess at a number you can look up in two minutes, and do not volunteer problems you have not fixed."},
{id:"u5l6-4",t:"u5l6",q:"A finding cites four devices. Your own review finds seven with the same problem. What should the corrective action plan say?",a:["Four, matching the finding, so the plan is not seen as an admission of a wider failure","Four now, with the remaining three handled quietly afterward","Seven, because the scope of a finding almost always exceeds what was cited and the plan must address all of it","Four, with a note that other devices are being reviewed"],c:2,e:"Scope is one of the required parts of the plan, and understating it guarantees a repeat finding. The plan also needs root cause, corrective and preventive action, owners, dates, evidence of completion, and a sustainment measure."},
{id:"u5l6-5",t:"u5l6",q:"A draft corrective action plan gives the root cause as technician error. Why will that be rejected?",a:["Because root cause must always be a documentation failure","Because errors by individuals are not reportable findings","Because the technician was not interviewed","Because a root cause is a process rather than a person, and a plan built on blame produces no preventive action"],c:3,e:"A cause of technician error leads nowhere: there is no preventive action that applies the fix everywhere the same cause acts. The usable causes are missing procedures, missing checks, system configuration, training gaps, and workload."},
{id:"u5l6-6",t:"u5l6",q:"Every item in a corrective action plan has been completed on its date. What remains to be done?",a:["Report a sustainment measure to the environment of care committee for an agreed period, to show the correction held","Nothing further, once the evidence is filed","Request a re-survey to confirm closure","Revise the equipment management plan in full"],c:0,e:"Evidence of completion proves the fix happened; the sustainment measure proves it is still happening. Reporting it to the committee for an agreed number of months is what closes the loop and creates the record for the next survey."},

// u6l1
{id:"u6l1-1",t:"u6l1",q:"A capital request names a specific manufacturer and model in its first sentence, with no functional requirements stated. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Return it to the department to state the clinical requirement in functional terms, so the evaluation can compare models against those requirements","Obtain two further quotations from competing vendors","Approve it if the price is within the department's allocation","Ask purchasing whether that manufacturer is on contract"],c:0,e:"A request that specifies a product in advance is unfinished. Where only one product genuinely meets a clinical need, that conclusion is defensible and goes in writing with the reasoning; otherwise you have prevented a sole-source decision made in a sales meeting."},
{id:"u6l1-2",t:"u6l1",q:"Three machines are quoted at $558,000 and your analysis puts the ten-year cost of ownership at $726,700. What does that gap represent?",a:["Vendor price inflation over the life of the contract","The site work, training, test equipment, consumables, and disposal that the requester never costed, which is the main reason funded projects run over","Interest on the capital borrowing","The depreciation the hospital will record"],c:1,e:"Total cost of ownership is purchase plus installation and site preparation plus service plus parts plus consumables plus training plus disposal across the life. Finding the omitted costs is what the department's review exists to do."},
{id:"u6l1-3",t:"u6l1",q:"Months after the decision, the committee asks why the monitors were deferred while the ultrasound was funded. What should your paperwork already contain?",a:["The minutes of the meeting at which the decision was taken","The quotations for both requests","The score sheet with its published factors and weights, the values, the date and author, and a statement of what the hospital carries by deferring each unfunded item","The clinical departments' original written requests"],c:2,e:"The committee's real question is never whether a device is worthwhile but why this one before that one. Published weights scored before ranking answer it on one page, and the consequence statement explains what was accepted by deferring."},
{id:"u6l1-4",t:"u6l1",q:"Which costs are MOST often missing from a clinical department's capital request?",a:["The purchase price and the applicable taxes","The vendor's warranty and extended coverage options","Depreciation and the cost of capital","Installation, site preparation, removal of the old equipment, training, test equipment, consumables, renovation, and disposal"],c:3,e:"Requesters cost what they were quoted. Reviewing requests to identify omitted costs that are not apparent to the end user is one of the clearest contributions an equipment manager makes to the capital process."},
{id:"u6l1-5",t:"u6l1",q:"Two of six requests were declined this cycle. What is the BEST way to communicate that?",a:["Tell each requester the reason and what would change the answer next cycle","Inform the committee only, since the decision was theirs","Wait until the next cycle opens and invite them to resubmit","Send the ranked list to all requesters without further comment"],c:0,e:"A requester who understands the reason returns next year with a better case rather than a complaint, and the department keeps the clinical relationships it needs. Feedback also protects the credibility of the scoring process."},
{id:"u6l1-6",t:"u6l1",q:"A successor manager asks why the hospital owns a particular model from a particular vendor. Which file should answer that?",a:["The purchase order and the vendor's invoice","The evaluation record: the request, the clinical statement, the options compared, the cost model with its assumptions, the dated score sheet, the recommendation, and the decision with the body that made it","The asset record in the work order system","The contract file held by purchasing"],c:1,e:"The evaluation record answers the three questions nobody asks until years later: why this device, why this vendor, and why not the other request. It is also what satisfies an auditor."},

// u6l2
{id:"u6l2-1",t:"u6l2",q:"A large analyzer arrives and will not pass through the final doorway into its room. Which step was skipped?",a:["The vendor's pre-delivery notification","A site survey performed in the room, including walking the delivery route from the dock to the final position","The incoming inspection","The infection control risk assessment"],c:1,e:"The survey is done in the room with the utility owners present, before the purchase order, using the manufacturer's site planning documents and a tape measure. The delivery path is walked rather than read off a drawing."},
{id:"u6l2-2",t:"u6l2",q:"Your site survey lists device delivery at ten weeks, a network drop at three weeks, and supplementary cooling at twelve weeks. What sets the earliest possible go-live?",a:["The device delivery, since nothing can happen without the device","The network drop, because the interface cannot be tested without it","The supplementary cooling, because the longest-lead item on the critical path decides the date","Operator training, which always comes last"],c:2,e:"Long-lead site items routinely outlast the device itself: shielded rooms, new circuits, cooling capacity, and a drop in a finished ceiling. Identifying them is a main purpose of running the survey before the order."},
{id:"u6l2-3",t:"u6l2",q:"The sign-off sheet for a project has every line signed except the one for water supply and drainage. How should that line be treated?",a:["As satisfied, since nobody raised an objection","As a minor item that can be closed after installation","As the vendor's responsibility under the purchase agreement","As an assumption, which is what arrives on a delivery day as a problem"],c:3,e:"An unsigned requirement is an assumption. The sheet attached to the project file is also the document that settles who pays when something was missed, which is why each line carries one named owner."},
{id:"u6l2-4",t:"u6l2",q:"A dedicated circuit was not identified until after the walls were closed. What is the typical consequence?",a:["Several times the planned cost, a shutdown window nobody has, and idle device time while it is arranged","A minor delay with no cost impact","A warranty claim against the manufacturer","A finding at the next accreditation survey"],c:0,e:"The cheapest hour in any project is the one spent in the room before the order is placed. Electrical, water, and cooling work retrofitted into a finished space costs money, time, and credibility, in that order."},
{id:"u6l2-5",t:"u6l2",q:"On a project sign-off sheet, which requirement can only be signed by the equipment manager?",a:["Floor loading and structural capacity","Serviceability: parts, training, test equipment, and whether the hospital can maintain the device","Network addresses and bandwidth","Infection control during the construction work"],c:1,e:"Facilities, information technology, infection control, and the clinical department each own requirements you cannot sign for them. Whether the hospital can actually service and sustain the device is the one nobody else can assess."},
{id:"u6l2-6",t:"u6l2",q:"When should the information security assessment for a networked device take place?",a:["At installation, once the device's network details are known","After acceptance testing, before clinical use","During feasibility, before purchase, alongside the agreement with information technology","At the first patch cycle after go-live"],c:2,e:"The security review belongs in the feasibility stage with the other requirements, because a go-live blocked at the last moment by the one person who was never asked is a predictable and expensive failure."},

// u6l3
{id:"u6l3-1",t:"u6l3",q:"A shortlisted vendor confirms in writing that it does not sell parts to equipment owners and offers no service training. What does that answer decide?",a:["That the vendor must be eliminated from consideration","That the purchase price should be negotiated downward","That the service strategy is now a contract for the life of the device, which has to be priced into the request rather than discovered in year three","That the device requires an alternative maintenance program"],c:2,e:"A no on parts or training is not automatically disqualifying, but it removes in-house service as an option permanently and commits the hospital to a contract at a price it will never again be able to negotiate."},
{id:"u6l3-2",t:"u6l3",q:"You are searching the public device report database on a model you are considering. What is the correct way to read what you find?",a:["Count the records and prefer the model with fewer","Treat an absence of reports as proof the model is reliable","Treat the most serious single report as the verdict on the model","Read the narratives for repeated failure modes and pair them with the model's recall history, remembering that a widely used model naturally generates more reports"],c:3,e:"The database holds mandatory reports from manufacturers, importers, and user facilities plus voluntary reports, and anyone can search it. Patterns matter and raw counts mislead, and a new model simply has no history yet."},
{id:"u6l3-3",t:"u6l3",q:"A device has an expected clinical life of ten years and its embedded operating system is supported for four. What should the capital request say?",a:["That the device carries a known replacement or migration cost at year four, which belongs in the capital plan from the day it arrives","Nothing, since operating system support is the vendor's concern","That the device should be kept off the network","That the hospital should require a lifetime support commitment before purchase"],c:0,e:"Asking how long the operating system is supported, and what happens to the device when that support ends, turns a future surprise into a planned item. The answer belongs in the request alongside the security disclosure and the patching method."},
{id:"u6l3-4",t:"u6l3",q:"You are comparing a seven-year contract against seven years of in-house service. What rule keeps the comparison fair?",a:["Use the vendor's own cost projection for both options","Evaluate each option over the same period with identical assumptions, and show a best case and a worst case for each","Exclude training from the in-house option, since it is a one-time cost","Compare only the first three years, since later costs are speculative"],c:1,e:"Price each option individually over the same time frame, keep the variables identical across options, and state the hospital's maximum exposure as well as its expected cost so the committee can see the worst case it is accepting."},
{id:"u6l3-5",t:"u6l3",q:"Service manuals, software updates, passwords, and diagnostic access all need to be secured for a new purchase. How should they appear in the agreement?",a:["In the terms and conditions, where they bind the vendor legally","In a side letter from the vendor's service manager","As named deliverables with dates, with payment arranged to be withheld until they actually arrive","In the warranty documentation supplied at delivery"],c:2,e:"An invoice normally clears once the priced items are delivered and signed for, so unpriced promises inside the terms are the ones that quietly go missing. Naming each as a deliverable is what makes withholding payment possible."},
{id:"u6l3-6",t:"u6l3",q:"One shortlisted vendor has a single installed site within a hundred miles and no published support policy. Why does that matter to the service strategy?",a:["It affects the purchase price negotiation only","It requires the hospital to stock a complete set of spare parts","It prevents the device from entering an alternative maintenance program","Response time, travel cost, and the hospital's exposure if the product line is sold or discontinued all worsen, which pushes the strategy toward a contract or away from the vendor"],c:3,e:"Vendor stability, installed base nearby, local service presence, and what happens to support if the line changes hands are part of the sustainability assessment, because they decide what service will actually cost and how long it will take."},

// u6l4
{id:"u6l4-1",t:"u6l4",q:"Two pump models are 26 percent of the fleet and generate 67 percent of the use error work orders. Which argument should lead your case to the committee?",a:["Parts inventory reduction, which is the most easily quantified benefit","The reduction in training hours for technical staff","Contract leverage with a single supplier","Patient safety, because one interface and one alarm behavior reduce use error, especially for floating and agency staff"],c:3,e:"Equipment variation in a clinical area is a known contributor to use error, and the safety argument is both the strongest with a committee and the honest one. The cost benefits follow it rather than leading."},
{id:"u6l4-2",t:"u6l4",q:"Your standardization business case shows an annual saving of $46,000. What else must it state?",a:["The transition cost of replacing working devices, retraining operators, and running two fleets during the changeover, plus the risk accepted by single-sourcing","The vendor's discount schedule and payment terms","The expected useful life of the chosen model","The cost of service ratio for each model in the fleet"],c:0,e:"A case that presents standardization as free loses credibility at the first question. Naming the transition cost and the accepted risk is what keeps the committee's trust, which is worth more than the project."},
{id:"u6l4-3",t:"u6l4",q:"The hospital has standardized its entire pump fleet with one supplier. What risk has that concentrated?",a:["Higher purchase prices, since competition has been removed","A single recall, price increase, supply interruption, or end-of-support announcement now affects every device at once, and the supplier knows your switching cost","Reduced technician competency across other models","Longer parts lead times from a single source"],c:1,e:"Standardization is a policy rather than a religion. Its limits are genuine specialty needs, vendor lock-in with concentrated recall and supply risk, and obsolescence, and the usual mitigation is a residual second fleet on named units."},
{id:"u6l4-4",t:"u6l4",q:"A device standard is published with no route for exceptions. What does that produce?",a:["Complete consistency, which is the purpose of a standard","Faster purchasing decisions","Quiet non-compliance, as departments acquire what they need outside the process and devices arrive without an incoming inspection","A reduction in capital requests"],c:2,e:"A standard with no exception route produces workarounds rather than consistency, and workarounds arrive untagged and uninspected. Publish who requests, what evidence is required, who decides, and the review date for every exception granted."},
{id:"u6l4-5",t:"u6l4",q:"Capital for device standardization is unavailable this year. Where can the same argument still be applied quickly?",a:["To test equipment, by buying one analyzer type","To service contracts, by consolidating them with one vendor","To the alternative maintenance program, by using one interval for all models","To accessories and consumables such as cables, probes, cuffs, circuits, sets, and batteries, which drive much of the cost, downtime, and user frustration"],c:3,e:"Standardizing below the device is cheaper and faster than standardizing capital, and it borrows the pharmacy's idea: a published list of approved devices and accessories, maintained by a committee, enforced by purchasing, with a defined route in and out."},
{id:"u6l4-6",t:"u6l4",q:"A proposal would standardize the hospital onto a model whose manufacturer has announced support ending in three years. What is the objection?",a:["Standardizing onto a model late in its life locks the hospital into an obsolescence date for the whole fleet","The purchase price will not be competitive","Technicians would need retraining twice","The model cannot be placed in an alternative maintenance program"],c:0,e:"Confirming the support horizon is part of the decision. A standard adopted near end of life commits the hospital to replacing everything at once, which is the opposite of what the standardization case promised."},

// u6l5
{id:"u6l5-1",t:"u6l5",q:"A vendor engineer worked on a device for three hours on a Saturday and left no service report, and the device later failed during a case. What control would have prevented the gap?",a:["Paying for outside service from the department's budget, so payment can be withheld until a check-in, a check-out, and a service report within 24 hours are received","Requiring the vendor to call the on-call technician before arriving","Prohibiting vendor work outside normal hours","Asking the clinical department to record the visit"],c:0,e:"The hospital is accountable for the whole maintenance history, including work others performed. Control of payment is the only real leverage, and the requirement is written into contracts and purchase orders so it can be enforced."},
{id:"u6l5-2",t:"u6l5",q:"Two of six ordered accessories and a service software key have not arrived with a new device. When is the hospital's leverage strongest?",a:["At the first service call, when the missing items become urgent","At acceptance, before payment, because an invoice clears once the priced items are signed for","At the first contract renewal","At the end of the warranty period"],c:1,e:"Incoming inspection and acceptance testing is the last moment the hospital holds the money. A device put into clinical use before that record exists is both a safety exposure and a lost negotiation."},
{id:"u6l5-3",t:"u6l5",q:"A contracted company sends technicians to service your imaging equipment. Who is responsible for ensuring those people are competent?",a:["The contracting company alone, under the terms of its agreement","The clinical department that uses the equipment","The equipment manager, who samples the company's training records and competency assessments","The accrediting organization, during its survey"],c:2,e:"The duty to ensure that everyone working on medical equipment is qualified does not transfer with the contract. The hospital keeps records of its own people's qualifications and must demonstrate how it assures contracted personnel are qualified."},
{id:"u6l5-4",t:"u6l5",q:"Vendors frequently arrive after your department's working hours and resist walking to the shop to sign in. What is the BEST practical answer?",a:["Extend the department's hours to cover vendor arrivals","Require all vendor work to be scheduled during normal hours","Drop the check-in requirement for contract vendors","Allow check-in by email to a named control person with a copy to you, and write that requirement into contracts and purchase orders"],c:3,e:"Physical check-in fails because it is out of the vendor's way, costs them billable time, and often falls outside your hours. An email process to a named person, backed by payment control and contract language, actually works."},
{id:"u6l5-5",t:"u6l5",q:"A service agreement comes up for renewal in sixty days. Which document is MOST useful in that conversation?",a:["A scorecard built from your own records: response and arrival commitments met, scheduled visits delivered, reports received on time, repeat visits, parts lead times, and check-in compliance","The vendor's own annual report of parts and labor delivered","The published cost of service band for that modality","The clinical department's satisfaction survey results"],c:0,e:"The vendor's report is priced at list and written to justify renewal. Your own records turn a renewal into a negotiation, and a vendor that knows it is measured behaves differently from one that does not."},
{id:"u6l5-6",t:"u6l5",q:"When should a vendor escalation path with names and timescales for both sides be agreed?",a:["At the first missed commitment, when the need is demonstrable","At contract signature, published on both sides and used in order","At each annual review","When the department director becomes involved"],c:1,e:"An escalation path invented during an outage is just a series of phone calls. Agreed at signature, it runs from technician to service engineer up to payment being withheld or cancellation considered, and skipping levels costs the relationship."},

// u6l6
{id:"u6l6-1",t:"u6l6",q:"In a project schedule, which tasks slipping by a week will move the go-live date?",a:["Any task with an external owner","Tasks on the critical path, the longest chain of dependent tasks through the schedule","Tasks with the largest budget attached","Tasks scheduled in the final two weeks"],c:1,e:"A slip on the critical path slips the finish; tasks off it have slack. Knowing which is which is what lets a manager tell a sponsor honestly whether a particular delay matters."},
{id:"u6l6-2",t:"u6l6",q:"On the morning of a planned go-live, night shift operator training stands at 80 percent complete. What is the BEST decision?",a:["Go live and complete the remaining training during the first week","Go live on day shift only and leave the old devices in place at night","Hold the gate for two days until training is complete and recorded","Delegate the remaining training to the unit's charge nurses"],c:2,e:"Readiness is a gate rather than a wish list. Two days lost is cheaper than an incident on an untrained shift, and a sponsor remembers a held gate as competence rather than delay."},
{id:"u6l6-3",t:"u6l6",q:"A clinical department asks for two extra devices and a changed layout after the project plan is baselined. What is the correct route?",a:["Absorb the request if the budget allows, to maintain the relationship","A new project for the additional scope","A note in the project minutes for action at close-out","A change request carrying the cost and schedule impact, so the sponsor decides rather than discovers"],c:3,e:"The change request is the formal route to alter scope, schedule, or budget after baselining, and pricing the impact is what makes it a decision. Scope creep is what happens in the absence of that route."},
{id:"u6l6-4",t:"u6l6",q:"Which set of items belongs in the sustainment plan before a project can be closed?",a:["Assets inventoried with cost, owner, risk score, and schedule; procedures and intervals; parts stock; training delivered and recorded; service strategy in force; software versions and a tested backup; security settled; documentation filed; old assets retired","The final invoice, the warranty certificate, and the vendor's contact details","The acceptance test record and the training attendance sheet","The project charter, schedule, and risk register, archived"],c:0,e:"The sustainment plan is what makes the device supportable the day after the vendor leaves. Close-out then compares final cost against budget, retires the old assets, and records lessons learned."},
{id:"u6l6-5",t:"u6l6",q:"Your lessons-learned file from the last three projects has never been opened again. What would make it worth the hour it takes?",a:["Circulating it to all stakeholders after each project","Using each lesson to change something durable: a line in the site survey template, a clause in the next contract, or a task on the project checklist","Presenting it to the environment of care committee","Storing it with the project's financial records"],c:1,e:"Lessons learned are worth an hour only if they change a template, a checklist, or a contract term. Anything else is a file that documents a mistake without preventing its repetition."},
{id:"u6l6-6",t:"u6l6",q:"Most hospital equipment projects are not limited by technical difficulty. What limits them, and what is the BEST response?",a:["Budget, answered by phasing the work across two fiscal years","Vendor delivery times, answered by ordering earlier","Other departments' dependencies and calendars, answered by putting a representative from each in the project and publishing a weekly one-page status naming the decisions needed and their dates","Clinical resistance, answered by executive sponsorship"],c:2,e:"Facilities, information technology, materials management, infection control, education, and the clinical department own most of the critical-path tasks. Making a slipping dependency visible to its owner and the sponsor at the same time is the control that works."},

// u6l7
{id:"u6l7-1",t:"u6l7",q:"A utility notice names the third floor east wing, but two rooms on the second floor are fed from the same riser. What does this teach about handling notices?",a:["Notices should be circulated more widely within the hospital","Facilities should issue notices earlier","The named area is a starting point, and the actual scope must be confirmed with facilities before the device list is built","Each clinical unit should assess its own exposure"],c:2,e:"One named owner reads every utility notice, with a backup, and converts it into a device question: which rooms, which devices, which systems, including the ones fed from that system outside the area the notice mentioned."},
{id:"u6l7-2",t:"u6l7",q:"Power is restored after a planned interruption and the devices come back on. What must be verified before any of them returns to a patient?",a:["That each device powers up without an error message","That the utility notice has been filed with the project records","That the clinical unit is satisfied with the restoration","Power-up and self-test, settings and alarm limits intact, correct date and time, network connection and data flowing, and batteries charging"],c:3,e:"A device that was off is not a device that is working. Settings, alarm limits, time stamps, and network connections are exactly what a power event quietly discards, and the failure mode is silent rather than obvious."},
{id:"u6l7-3",t:"u6l7",q:"The equipment manager is invited into a construction project only after the drawings are fixed. What is the predictable consequence?",a:["Equipment decisions already made on the drawings become construction costs nobody budgeted, or a finished room that will not hold the device the department ordered","The project schedule will slip by a few weeks","Infection control will object to the construction plan","The vendor will refuse to install the equipment"],c:0,e:"Attendance at planning and design meetings is where an equipment list with its space, power, network, and environmental requirements is cheap to apply. Afterward it becomes a change order or a room that does not work."},
{id:"u6l7-4",t:"u6l7",q:"During construction, life safety features in an area are temporarily impaired. What are the compensating arrangements that apply called?",a:["The infection control risk assessment","Interim life safety measures","The utility interruption plan","The emergency operations plan"],c:1,e:"Interim life safety measures are the temporary compensations in force while life safety features are impaired by work. The infection control risk assessment separately sets containment, airflow, and work-hour controls for the project."},
{id:"u6l7-5",t:"u6l7",q:"Which half of a downtime procedure is MOST often missing, and why does it matter?",a:["The declaration, because nobody knows who may declare downtime","The notification list, because contact details go out of date","Recovery and reconciliation, because that is where most of the harm occurs: who declares the system restored, how downtime data is reconciled, and how each device is verified before it returns to a patient","The paper forms, because they are not stocked on the units"],c:2,e:"Write recovery with the same care as the outage. The procedure must also say how the backlog is entered afterward and how a recall or advisory in progress is picked back up."},
{id:"u6l7-6",t:"u6l7",q:"A proposed network change will affect a group of clinical devices. What must the change record contain for the change to be reversible?",a:["The vendor's written approval and the clinical department's agreement","The change board's minutes and the scheduled window","A risk assessment signed by the security officer","A back-out plan tested before the window, and the version and result recorded against each affected asset"],c:3,e:"The pattern behind nearly every self-inflicted clinical outage is a reasonable change made quietly, after which nobody could say what had changed and so nobody could put it back. The back-out plan and the version record are what prevent that."},

// u6l8
{id:"u6l8-1",t:"u6l8",q:"A nursing director says her unit never hears anything from your department, while your records show eleven work orders closed on target last month. What is happening?",a:["The records are inaccurate and need auditing","The work orders were closed against the wrong unit","The director is reporting a complaint on behalf of one nurse","The work happened and the communication did not, and in her experience those are the same thing"],c:3,e:"Closing the loop is part of the work order, and perception is built by rounding, visible follow-up, and telling people what was found. Both parties in this conversation are telling the truth."},
{id:"u6l8-2",t:"u6l8",q:"What is the purpose of rounding on clinical units without a work order in hand?",a:["To surface problems nobody reported, check a sample of asset tags, and build the relationship, then act on something visible and come back and say what you did","To verify that scheduled maintenance has been completed","To collect satisfaction survey responses in person","To identify devices that should be removed from service"],c:0,e:"Rounding finds unreported faults, devices stored in ways that shorten their life, training gaps, and equipment nobody told you about. Most of what it finds would never have reached a work order."},
{id:"u6l8-3",t:"u6l8",q:"What belongs in a fifteen-minute daily huddle?",a:["A review of the month's performance metrics against goals","Yesterday's escalations, today's priorities, who is blocked, who is out, and which units need attention","Policy and procedure changes and training plans","Individual performance feedback for each technician"],c:1,e:"The huddle handles today. Numbers and goals belong in the monthly shop meeting, performance feedback belongs in a monthly one to one, and neither should first appear in an annual review."},
{id:"u6l8-4",t:"u6l8",q:"What should meeting minutes record, and what should happen at the start of the next meeting?",a:["A full account of the discussion, reviewed for accuracy at the next meeting","Attendance and the agenda items covered, filed for the record","Decisions, actions, owners, and dates, with the previous meeting's action list read out first","The chair's summary, circulated for comment before the next meeting"],c:2,e:"Minutes are not a transcript; the only lines that matter are the ones somebody has to act on. An action list that is never revisited trains everybody to ignore it."},
{id:"u6l8-5",t:"u6l8",q:"A vendor has missed its on-site response commitment three times on one asset. What makes a written escalation effective?",a:["A clear statement of how the failures have affected the department's reputation","A meeting request with the vendor's regional manager","A request for a credit against the next invoice","The dates and times from your own records, the contract clause by number, the clinical impact, a specific action by a specific date, and the next escalation level, copied to purchasing"],c:3,e:"Facts, clause, impact, request, date. Frustration persuades nobody and weakens a position the records would have won on their own, and copying purchasing keeps the commercial route open."},
{id:"u6l8-6",t:"u6l8",q:"A vendor has begun negotiating a service arrangement directly with a clinical director, bypassing your department. What is the BEST response?",a:["Bring the conversation back into a scheduled meeting with purchasing present, so commercial traffic runs through the agreed route","Raise it with the clinical director privately and ask them to decline","Report the vendor to the corporate compliance office","Decline to support any arrangement the vendor reaches"],c:0,e:"Routine technical traffic runs between technician and service engineer, while commercial traffic, performance problems, and anything involving money run through the manager and purchasing. Letting a vendor negotiate around you is how unserviceable commitments get signed."},

// u6l9
{id:"u6l9-1",t:"u6l9",q:"After a weekend monitoring upgrade, vital signs stop reaching the patient record. The monitors, the network, and the record system all test as working. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["The upgrade changed something on one side of an interface that nobody owned, and the other side was not told or tested","A network segment misconfiguration that only affects data traffic","A licensing failure in the record system","Device clocks drifting out of synchronization"],c:0,e:"An interface is a thing somebody has to own, not a space between two owners. The root cause of an integration failure is almost never the software; it is a change on one side without the other side being told, tested, or asked."},
{id:"u6l9-2",t:"u6l9",q:"What is the characteristic failure mode of a device-to-record integration?",a:["An error message in the device log","Data quietly stops arriving with no error anywhere, so somebody has to be monitoring for absence","A duplicate record created for each transmission","A sudden increase in network traffic"],c:1,e:"The common failure is silence rather than an error, which is why the integration register names somebody who would notice. Include a test that disconnects a device and confirms monitoring notices within the agreed time."},
{id:"u6l9-3",t:"u6l9",q:"Following a device software update, what should the post-change test confirm?",a:["That the device reconnects to the network and the link shows as active","That the device passes its own self-test","That a known value sent from the device arrives in the record unaltered, on the right patient, with the right units, time stamp, and device identity","That the vendor has recorded the update in its service report"],c:2,e:"Test the data rather than the connection. An interface that is up and delivering the wrong field, the wrong units, or the wrong patient is considerably more dangerous than one that is plainly down."},
{id:"u6l9-4",t:"u6l9",q:"A nurse reports that a set of vital signs appeared in the wrong patient's record. How should this be handled?",a:["As a device fault, investigated and repaired by the department","As a documentation error for the unit to correct","As an information technology incident, escalated to the network team","As a patient safety event, reported and investigated as one, with evidence preserved on both sides of the interface and both owners in the investigation"],c:3,e:"A wrong-patient or wrong-value result is a patient safety event rather than a technical fault, and it needs the same rigor as a device failure. Both interface owners belong in the investigation because the cause usually sits between them."},
{id:"u6l9-5",t:"u6l9",q:"Before a significant technology decision, which four groups should be consulted?",a:["Users, management, vendors, and your own technicians","Senior leadership, finance, purchasing, and the vendor","Nursing, information technology, facilities, and infection control","The capital committee, the clinical director, the vendor, and the accreditor"],c:0,e:"Users know what the workflow actually is, management knows direction and funding, vendors know what the product can and cannot do, and technicians know what fails and what is unserviceable. A requirement list from a single source does not survive contact with reality."},
{id:"u6l9-6",t:"u6l9",q:"What should the written ownership agreement between your department and information technology settle?",a:["The budget split for network equipment serving clinical devices","Who owns the device, who owns the network, who may change a configuration, which change windows apply to clinical devices, who approves vendor remote access, and the joint incident path","Which department reports networked device failures to the committees","The naming convention for device network addresses"],c:1,e:"Agreeing the boundary in writing and having both directors sign it avoids the argument that otherwise happens at two in the morning with a patient on the other end of it."}
);
